WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
Cross-attention.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:06.000
What if the questions come from one sequence, and the answers from another?

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.900
That is the whole definition.

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.967
Queries come from one sequence.

5
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:13.467
Keys and values come from a different one.

6
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.367
Compare it with self-attention.

7
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.033
In self-attention, all three come from the same sequence.

8
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:25.567
In cross-attention, the queries come from one side and the keys and values from the other.

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:27.267
The math is the same.

10
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:28.867
Only the wiring changes.

11
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.733
Here is how it plays out in a model like T5.

12
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.100
The encoder reads the whole source log.

13
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.262
Every decoder layer then asks questions of it.

14
00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:41.933
Which part of the source answers the word I am writing now?

15
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:47.333
The decoder's queries reach into the encoder's keys and values, and the next word comes out.

16
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.800
The same wiring shows up wherever two streams must talk.

17
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.767
Encoder-decoder models use it for summaries.

18
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:59.067
Multimodal models let text queries attend over image patches.

19
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:03.800
And diffusion models inject the text prompt into image generation this way.

20
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.367
Let's look inside a real T5 model.

21
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.333
We encode a short source text.

22
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:12.933
Then we generate while asking for the cross-attention maps.

23
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:18.600
We take the first step of the last layer, average the heads, and find the strongest source token.

24
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.100
Finally we print which source word the decoder was looking at.

25
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.100
Here is a real use: captioning wildfire satellite images.

26
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.567
The model writes: smoke plume in the northeast quadrant.

27
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:36.033
The cross-attention map over image patches shows which pixels justified that phrase.

28
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:41.667
Review tooling uses those maps to catch captions the model wrote without actually looking.

29
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:43.617
Let's recap.

30
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:47.433
Queries come from one sequence, keys and values from another.

31
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:50.733
Each decoder layer asks questions of the encoder.

32
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:54.200
The same trick powers multimodal and diffusion models.

33
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.040
And the maps show what the model looked at.

34
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:57.867
Next up: BERT.
